---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import StatusTag from '../components/StatusTag.astro';
import { status } from '../lib/status';

const description =
  'The ITISYOU OS system platform (V0.7-V0.10): capability-based permissions with default deny, system services started by a userspace init and supervised, a managed application model with Ed25519-signed packages trusted through an offline-root key hierarchy, atomic updates with rollback and recovery, capability-scoped userspace filesystem writes, port-scoped network access, and a hash-chained audit trail that survives reboots — every claim backed by QEMU test evidence.';

/** Capability table — mirrors kernel_core::caps (the enforced source). */
const caps = [
  ['spawn', 'create + wait on child processes'],
  ['ipc', 'message-channel send/receive'],
  ['gui', 'window create/draw/present'],
  ['dev', 'device-table queries'],
  ['fs_read', 'filesystem reads (within the sandbox prefixes)'],
  ['fs_write', 'filesystem writes to the persistent store (V0.8)'],
  ['network', 'UDP sockets and DNS — scoped to a port (V0.8); TCP connect and listen — scoped to the port the connection is opened for (V0.9)'],
  ['audio', 'reserved — no userspace audio syscall yet'],
  ['sys_admin', 'reserved — userspace service/package control'],
  ['proc_control', 'reserved — process teardown and control beyond spawning and waiting (the process kind’s CONTROL right: ending an existing process). No syscall checks it yet: Ring 3 has no kill, and kill is a kernel-console command'],
  ['service','the supervisor report to the kernel’s service table (svc_report, V0.10); in v1.0.0 also init’s kernel mailbox (init_ctl), which in addition requires the caller to be the live /sbin/init'],
  ['sys_view', 'the approved read-only system view (sys_view) — V0.11, shipped in v1.0.0. Console-granted only: in no default set, never delegated to a child, refused in /etc/init.conf'],
  ['propose', 'filing a proposal for the console to approve or deny (propose) — V0.11, shipped in v1.0.0. Console-granted only, like sys_view; a proposal grants nothing by itself'],
];

const evidence = [
  [
    'Default deny',
    'A probe launched with ZERO capabilities attempts all 7 privileged syscall classes; every one must return ERR_PERM.',
    'SANDBOX-DENIED-OK (exit 0 only if all attempts were denied)',
  ],
  [
    'No amplification',
    'A parent holding only {spawn, fs_read} requests gui for its child; the kernel intersects — the child finds gui denied while its delegated fs_read works.',
    'CAP-DELEGATION-OK',
  ],
  [
    'Sandbox traversal defense',
    'A process sandboxed to /etc attempts /bin, a foreign app dir, /etc/../bin/init, and a root escape — all denied on the NORMALIZED path; an in-sandbox miss stays NOENT.',
    'FS-SANDBOX-OK',
  ],
  [
    'Service supervision',
    'echod serves a dependent client (3 IPC ping→pong); crashd faults, is contained, restarted exactly 3× by the bounded policy, then marked Failed. Startup order is dependency-driven and cycle-checked.',
    'ECHOD-SERVED-3 · SVC-CLIENT-OK · state=failed restarts=3',
  ],
  [
    'Package integrity',
    'A bit-flipped package (digest mismatch) and a digest-valid package demanding an undefined capability are both refused — with the store untouched. The digest is re-verified at every launch.',
    'verify result=refused reason=DigestMismatch',
  ],
  [
    'Manifest-only authority',
    'hello-app is granted exactly its manifest capability (fs_read works) and nothing more (its gui attempt is denied even though the binary asks).',
    'HELLO-APP-OK',
  ],
  [
    'Atomic update + rollback',
    'Updating stages the new version, then commits with ONE crash-atomic filesystem transition. Rollback removes the newest commit marker: the previous version reactivates and the demoted package is kept as evidence.',
    'rollback name=hello-app from=v2 to=v1 result=ok',
  ],
  [
    'Reboot-proven recovery',
    'Boot 1 installs v1 and stages v2 WITHOUT committing (a simulated crash mid-update), then powers off. Boot 2 finds v1 still active, detects the orphaned staged update, removes it via the recovery scan, and launches v1.',
    '[ITISYOU:RECOVERY] orphan_staged=v2 action=remove',
  ],
  [
    'Audit trail',
    'Every privileged action and every capability denial is recorded: sequence, tick, pid, action, capability, result. No payload contents are logged.',
    '[ITISYOU:AUDIT] seq=… pid=… action=… result=denied',
  ],
];
---

<Base title="System Platform" description={description}>
  <div class="max-w-(--container-max) mx-auto px-4 sm:px-margin pt-12 pb-8 flex flex-col gap-16">
    <header class="border-b border-line-soft pb-12">
      <Breadcrumbs
        class="mb-6"
        crumbs={[{ label: 'SYSTEM', href: '/' }, { label: 'PLATFORM' }]}
      />
      <h1 class="font-sans font-light text-[36px] sm:text-display-lg text-ink uppercase mb-6">
        System Platform
      </h1>
      <p class="font-sans text-headline-md text-meta-bright max-w-2xl border-l-2 border-ink pl-4 py-1">
        Authority is explicit, delegated narrowly, checked in the kernel, and audited — for every process, and for an
        AI agent equally.
      </p>
      <p class="font-mono text-mono-data text-muted mt-6">
        v{status.version} · milestone “{status.milestone}” · everything below is machine-verified in QEMU (ADR-0012)
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- ============ capabilities ============ -->
    <section class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-gutter" aria-labelledby="p1-title">
      <div class="md:col-span-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="p1-title" class="font-mono text-mono-label text-ink tracking-widest">01 // CAPABILITIES</h2>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright">
          Thirteen syscalls need no capability, because each acts only on the caller itself:
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">write</span> (fd 1, its console output),
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">exit</span>, <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">yield</span>,
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">getpid</span>, <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">uptime</span>,
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">args</span> (its own arguments),
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">sleep</span>, <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">udp_close</span> (a
          socket it already owns), and <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">cap_list</span>,
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">cap_check</span>, <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">cap_revoke</span>
          and <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">cap_restrict</span> (its own handles, which it can inspect, drop or
          narrow but never widen). The thirteenth, <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">console_read</span>, is limited
          instead to the one process the kernel has lent the console’s input; anyone else gets
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">ERR_PERM</span>, audited. <em>Every</em> other syscall is default deny:
          the caller must hold a live capability handle of the right kind, rights and scope, or it gets
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">ERR_PERM</span> and the refusal lands in the audit trail, enforced in the
          kernel’s syscall layer. Delegation only narrows: <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">spawn</span> inherits
          the parent's set exactly (in v1.0.0, minus the two console-only capabilities), and
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">spawn_caps</span> grants <em>parent ∩ requested</em>. A child can
          never hold what its parent lacked.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-8">
        <div class="table-scroll panel">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse min-w-[480px]">
            <caption class="sr-only">Defined capabilities</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col" class="text-left font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-widest uppercase text-muted bg-log border-b border-line px-4 py-3">Capability</th>
                <th scope="col" class="text-left font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-widest uppercase text-muted bg-log border-b border-line px-4 py-3">Grants</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {caps.map(([name, grants]) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line last:border-b-0">
                  <td class="font-mono text-mono-data text-meta px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap">{name}</td>
                  <td class="text-body-base text-body px-4 py-3">{grants}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright text-sm mt-4">
          Trusted kernel-shell launches keep a legacy-full set so earlier milestones' behavior is unchanged;
          <strong>platform launches are default-deny</strong> — an app receives only what its manifest requests and
          its launcher actually holds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ services ============ -->
    <section class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-gutter" aria-labelledby="p2-title">
      <div class="md:col-span-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="p2-title" class="font-mono text-mono-label text-ink tracking-widest">02 // SYSTEM SERVICES</h2>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright">
          Services are ordinary Ring 3 processes — no hidden privileged daemons. A static registry declares each
          service's binary, dependencies, and the <em>exact</em> capabilities it runs with. The supervisor starts them
          in a deterministic, cycle-checked dependency order, contains crashes, and applies a bounded restart policy
          (3 restarts, then Failed — no restart storms). The <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">svc</span> command
          shows the real state table.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright">
          Since v0.10.0 the persistent services are started by <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">/sbin/init</span>
          — pid 1, an ordinary Ring 3 program with fixed authority — from
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">/etc/init.conf</span>, each with exactly the capabilities the file gives
          it. Init reports every start, restart and failure to the kernel, which checks that the process is init's own
          child and prints the capabilities from its own record; if init dies, the kernel ends its services and starts
          a new init.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-8">
        <div class="log-block" aria-label="Service supervision evidence from a real QEMU run">
          <p class="text-muted">// real supervised run (QEMU serial evidence)</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] start name=echod pid=30 order=0 caps=0x2</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] start name=client pid=31 order=1 caps=0x2</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] start name=crashd pid=32 order=2 caps=0x0</p>
          <p>ECHOD-SERVED-3 · SVC-CLIENT-OK</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] name=crashd state=restarting pid=33 restarts=1</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] name=crashd state=restarting pid=34 restarts=2</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] name=crashd state=restarting pid=35 restarts=3</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] name=crashd state=failed pid=35 restarts=3</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:SVC] supervisor done started=3 done=2 failed=1 restarts=3</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ apps + packages + updates ============ -->
    <section class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-gutter" aria-labelledby="p3-title">
      <div class="md:col-span-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="p3-title" class="font-mono text-mono-label text-ink tracking-widest">03 // APPS, PACKAGES, UPDATES</h2>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright">
          An application ships as one <strong>ITPKG</strong> file: a strict manifest (name, version,
          <em>requested capabilities</em> — unknown keys or capabilities are errors, never silently tolerated) plus the
          ELF payload, integrity-protected by SHA-256 and re-verified at every launch. The persistent store keeps
          version-numbered packages with commit markers; install, update, rollback, and recovery are each
          <strong>one crash-atomic filesystem transition</strong> — an interrupted update can never activate.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright">
          Honesty note: SHA-256 is <em>integrity</em>, not authenticity, so since V0.8 a package also carries an
          Ed25519 signature, checked after the digest. Since v0.9.0 the kernel trusts only an offline root, and signing
          keys only through root-signed certificates limited by package-name scope and release-epoch validity, subject
          to a signed revocation list (ADR-0021). v0.8.1 checks against one compiled-in development key whose seed is
          published: the mechanism is real, that key anchors no trust.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-8">
        <div class="log-block" aria-label="Package lifecycle evidence from a real QEMU run">
          <p class="text-muted">// real lifecycle (QEMU serial evidence)</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:PKG] install name=hello-app v=1 result=ok</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:PKG] verify result=refused reason=DigestMismatch</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:PKG] launch name=hello-app v=1 caps=0x10 · HELLO-APP-OK</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:PKG] install name=hello-app v=2 result=ok</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:PKG] rollback name=hello-app from=v2 to=v1 result=ok</p>
          <p class="text-muted">// after a simulated crash mid-update + REAL reboot:</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:RECOVERY] app=hello-app orphan_staged=v2 action=remove</p>
          <p>[ITISYOU:PKG] launch name=hello-app v=1 · HELLO-APP-OK</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ verified security properties ============ -->
    <section aria-labelledby="p4-title">
      <h2 id="p4-title" class="font-mono text-mono-label text-ink tracking-widest mb-8">
        04 // VERIFIED SECURITY PROPERTIES
      </h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-px bg-line border border-line">
        {evidence.map(([title, body, marker]) => (
          <div class="bg-surface p-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
              <p class="font-sans text-body-lg text-ink">{title}</p>
              <StatusTag status="verified" class="shrink-0 mt-0.5" />
            </div>
            <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright text-sm">{body}</p>
            <p class="font-mono text-mono-data text-muted text-xs mt-auto break-words">{marker}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright text-sm mt-6 max-w-3xl">
        Each property above is asserted by the automated QEMU matrix (in-kernel selftests plus dedicated platform and
        two-boot update/recovery legs) on every CI run — a property is listed here only while its test stays green.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ AI authority ============ -->
    <section class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-gutter" aria-labelledby="p5-title">
      <div class="md:col-span-4">
        <h2 id="p5-title" class="font-mono text-mono-label text-ink tracking-widest">05 // AI ≠ AUTHORITY</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-8 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright max-w-3xl">
          The platform's privileged path — <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">request → identity → capability
          check → deterministic service → action → audit</span> — is the <em>only</em> privileged path. An AI agent
          gets no other entry point: it requests actions exactly like any untrusted application, subject to the same
          default-deny capabilities, the same sandbox, and the same audit trail. The released v0.10.0 image contains no
          AI component.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body-base text-meta-bright max-w-3xl">
          In v1.0.0, the first agent works exactly this
          way. It holds the view and the right to propose, both console-granted only, plus IPC and reads under
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">/etc/ai</span>; its own attempts at
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">svc_report</span>, <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">fs_write</span>
          and <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">spawn</span> are refused, and no syscall it can call performs either
          of its two actions — resuming the scheduler, retrying a failed service. Inference runs in Ring 3, in inferd under
          <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">/sbin/init</span>; the kernel only re-evaluates the pinned model on the
          view it served, to check the agent’s claim, and never acts on a model’s output. A proposal the kernel accepts
          waits for <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">approve</span> or <span class="font-mono text-mono-data">deny</span>
          at the console; an approved action runs in kernel code, is verified, is rolled back if the check fails, and
          each step is audited with the model and view digests the proposal cited.
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-mono-data text-muted">
          Source: <a href="/docs/security-model" class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-ink">docs/SECURITY_MODEL.md</a> ·
          ADR-0012 · evidence in the repository's <span class="font-mono">artifacts/qemu/</span> JSON + serial logs
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
